[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Request Building: Methods, Headers, and Body Payloads in Postman"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering request building is fundamental for effective API testing. In Postman, constructing a robust HTTP request involves selecting the correct HTTP method, defining appropriate headers, and structuring the body payload accurately."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Selecting HTTP Methods"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The HTTP method tells the server what action to perform on the resource. Postman supports all standard REST methods through a dropdown menu next to the request URL bar."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"GET: Retrieve data from a server without modifying it."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"POST: Send data to the server to create a new resource."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"PUT: Update an existing resource entirely or create it if it does not exist."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"PATCH: Apply partial modifications to a resource."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"DELETE: Remove a specified resource from the server."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Configuring Headers"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Headers provide metadata about the request, such as authentication tokens, content types, and client information. Navigate to the 'Headers' tab under the URL input field in Postman."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Content-Type: Tells the server what format the request body is in (e.g., application/json)."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Authorization: Used to pass credentials like Bearer tokens or Basic auth keys."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Accept: Specifies which response formats the client can process (e.g., application/json)."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Constructing Body Payloads"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"For methods like POST, PUT, and PATCH, you often need to send data in the request body. Postman provides multiple body types depending on your API's requirements."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"form-data: Used for sending key-value pairs, typically for file uploads."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"x-www-form-urlencoded: Similar to HTML form submissions."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"raw: Used for sending text, JSON, XML, or JavaScript data. JSON is the most common format."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"{
  "username": "johndoe",
  "email": "john@example.com",
  "role": "admin"
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"When using the 'raw' body type with JSON, ensure you set the 'Content-Type' header to 'application/json' so the server correctly parses the incoming payload."}]
